\title{Transformer Encoder}
\description{
A stack of \code{num_layers} transformer encoder layers as described in \emph{Attention Is All You Need},
see \code{\link[=mlr_pipeops_nn_transformer_encoder_layer]{nn("transformer_encoder_layer")}} for a single
one of them.

This is a thin wrapper around \code{\link[torch:nn_transformer_encoder]{torch::nn_transformer_encoder()}} that makes it usable as a
building block of a \code{\link[mlr3pipelines:Graph]{Graph}} of tensor operations.
}
\section{nn_module}{

Calls \code{\link[torch:nn_transformer_encoder]{torch::nn_transformer_encoder()}} when trained, where the encoder layer is built with
\code{\link[torch:nn_transformer_encoder_layer]{torch::nn_transformer_encoder_layer()}}, the parameter \code{d_model} is inferred as the last
dimension of the input tensor and \code{batch_first} is always \code{TRUE}, see section \emph{Tensor Layout}.

\code{torch} copies the encoder layer once per position of the stack, so the layers have independent
weights, but they all start from the values of the one layer that was initialized.
}

\section{Parameters}{

The parameters are those of
\code{\link[=mlr_pipeops_nn_transformer_encoder_layer]{nn("transformer_encoder_layer")}}, which apply to
every layer of the stack, and additionally:
\itemize{
\item \code{num_layers} :: \code{integer(1)}\cr
The number of encoder layers in the stack.
\item \code{norm} :: \code{logical(1)}\cr
Whether to apply a layer normalization to the output of the stack, which is what a pre-norm
stack (\code{norm_first = TRUE}) usually ends with. Default is \code{FALSE}.
}
}

\section{State}{

The state is the value calculated by the public method \verb{$shapes_out()}.
}

\section{Tensor Layout}{

Input and output are \verb{(batch, sequence, feature)}, i.e. the \code{batch_first} layout of
\code{\link[torch:nn_transformer_encoder_layer]{torch::nn_transformer_encoder_layer()}}, which is fixed and not a hyperparameter.
\code{torch} defaults to \verb{(sequence, batch, feature)}, but the first dimension of every shape has to
be the batch dimension here.

The layer preserves the shape of its input, so the output shape is the input shape.
Only the feature dimension has to be known, the sequence length may be \code{NA}.
}

\section{Input and Output Channels}{

There is always one input channel \code{"input"}, which is the sequence the layer attends over, and
one output channel \code{"output"} of the same shape.

The construction arguments \code{src_mask} and \code{src_key_padding_mask} each add a further input
channel of that name, which is passed to the corresponding argument of the module's \code{forward()}
method. They are construction arguments and not hyperparameters, because they determine the
structure of the \code{\link[mlr3pipelines:Graph]{Graph}}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"src_mask"}: an additive or boolean mask over the sequence, of shape \verb{(sequence, sequence)}
or \verb{(batch * nhead, sequence, sequence)}. Positions that are \code{TRUE} are \emph{not} attended to.
\item \code{"src_key_padding_mask"}: a mask of shape \verb{(batch, sequence)} marking the padding positions of
each observation, which are \code{TRUE}.
}

Note that a \verb{(sequence, sequence)} \code{"src_mask"} has no batch dimension, unlike every other tensor
in a network, so its first dimension is the sequence length rather than the batch size.

For an explanation see \code{\link{PipeOpTorch}}.
}

